B.dY7=new A.j(B.err,[B.bEO,B.dK_,B.bG7,B.drT,B.bVK,B.dOX,B.cm_,B.dSI,B.czQ,B.c7t,B.dSl,B.bZc,B.bXZ,B.cTo,B.dkS,B.daj,B.bL9,B.dDA,B.ctO,B.bW0,B.cqp,B.cHO,B.cTQ,B.cPI,B.bX8,B.bLf,B.dNv,B.dPh,B.d7L,B.dyh,B.cLh,B.cnl,B.dLY,B.cCj,B.cEA,B.cqR,B.cTr,B.cKC,B.deO,B.bKo,B.cBa,B.dRf,B.dzO,B.cp2,B.crb,B.dty,B.bYA,B.dpa,B.d9A,B.dMi,B.ceC,B.cT6,B.dgt,B.dOh,B.dc9,B.cx9,B.d93,B.cwA,B.cxJ,B.dj8,B.bFh,B.cG4,B.dJQ,B.d1n,B.c5Y,B.c8G,B.dFI,B.dcl,B.d6i,B.d8t,B.cIp,B.duh,B.dLU,B.cTd,B.cYT,B.bVg,B.deM,B.cGw,B.bE5,B.d6p,B.dRt,B.duT,B.dJM,B.bFY,B.dFc,B.da2,B.bIO,B.bCV,B.cTR,B.dyP,B.cFG,B.drf,B.dIz,B.dqC,B.cRl,B.dhb,B.die,B.cHs,B.cit,B.dxQ,B.cv9,B.bYq,B.cZ9,B.cns,B.c2_,B.dqE,B.c_w,B.dsX,B.dGN,B.dNK,B.d5l,B.dLA,B.dk3,B.c5G,B.dKI,B.cPU,B.bMC,B.bBZ,B.d7v,B.cfg,B.cTx,B.cWW,B.ckR,B.cwS,B.d5v,B.deu,B.cHf,B.d5S,B.cvl,B.ckA,B.cUC,B.dKn,B.bHa,B.dqm,B.ddH,B.bYr,B.cnf,B.d0U,B.d4c,B.dnn,B.dzu,B.dIc,B.dD1,B.cmi,B.cpj,B.cVa,B.cIA,B.cgI,B.cD6,B.czP,B.cgj,B.deH,B.d1g,B.bXl,B.dtl,B.bTC,B.bVB,B.clm,B.cfT,B.dmK,B.bHJ,B.c2A,B.cPq,B.dSL,B.dMk,B.bRk,B.cVP,B.bYv,B.bVc,B.cER,B.d8W,B.cIX,B.d40,B.bL0,B.c3b,B.cjD,B.cBF,B.bDg,B.deK,B.dit,B.dAy,B.cNA,B.dam,B.cIz,B.dvX,B.cDq,B.cRH,B.d7J,B.cbX,B.bOz,B.cLB,B.cUA,B.dnd,B.co_,B.bIf,B.cCK,B.dbf,B.cl6,B.cJI,B.dKT],t.mm)
B.bma=new A.IS(B.dD7,B.cVy,B.bZG,B.ebj,B.cW,B.eby,B.ebx,B.cW,B.dXk,B.dXl,B.aEe,B.dXE,B.dY7)
B.Qt=s(["\uc608\uc694","\uc774\uc608\uc694","\uc774\uc57c\uc694"],t.s)
B.aVX=new A.Q("ko:g-copula",0,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc774\uc5d0\uc694/\uc608\uc694","to be (am, is, are) after a noun","Use it to say that A is B, like I'm a student or this is my bag. Add -ieyo (\uc774\uc5d0\uc694) after a noun that ends in a consonant and -yeyo after a noun that ends in a vowel (haksaeng-ieyo (\ud559\uc0dd\uc774\uc5d0\uc694), uisa-yeyo (\uc758\uc0ac\uc608\uc694)). English needs am, is, or are every time, but Korean attaches this ending to the noun itself, and the subject is often dropped when it's clear.","\uc800\ub294 \ud559\uc0dd\uc774\uc5d0\uc694.","jeoneun haksaengieyo.","I'm a student.","TOPIK 1","Don't use -yeyo (\uc608\uc694) after a consonant: haksaeng-yeyo is wrong, haksaeng-ieyo is right (\ud559\uc0dd\uc774\uc5d0\uc694).",!0,"\uc774\uc5d0\uc694",B.Qt,!0,1)
B.cvf=s(["\uc740","\uc774","\uac00"],t.s)
B.aWs=new A.Q("ko:g-topic",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc740/\ub294","topic marker (as for ...)","Attach it to the thing you are talking about: -eun (\uc740) after a consonant and -neun (\ub294) after a vowel (jeo-neun (\uc800\ub294), oneul-eun (\uc624\ub298\uc740)). It sets the topic, like 'as for me...' or 'speaking of today...', and it often adds a feeling of contrast. English has no such particle, so learners tend to skip it or treat it as the plain subject marker.","\uc800\ub294 \ud55c\uad6d\uc5b4\ub97c \uacf5\ubd80\ud574\uc694.","jeoneun hangugeoreul gongbuhaeyo.","I study Korean.","TOPIK 1","It marks what you're talking about, not strictly who does the action, so don't think of it as just the English subject.",!0,"\ub294",B.cvf,!0,1)
B.bHn=s(["\uac00","\ub97c","\uc5d0"],t.s)
B.aWH=new A.Q("ko:g-subject",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc774/\uac00","subject marker (who or what does it)","Attach it to the person or thing that does the action or has the quality: -i (\uc774) after a consonant and -ga (\uac00) after a vowel (dongsaeng-i (\ub3d9\uc0dd\uc774), bi-ga (\ube44\uac00)). It often introduces new information, like 'it's raining' or 'a cat is sleeping'. English shows the subject by word order, but Korean needs a particle, and it drops the subject word completely when it's obvious. Note that jeo becomes je-ga (\uc81c\uac00) for \"I\" (je-ga, not jeo-ga).","\ub3d9\uc0dd\uc774 \ud559\uad50\uc5d0 \uac00\uc694.","dongsaengi hakgyoe gayo.","My younger sibling goes to school.","TOPIK 1","Use -i/-ga (\uc774/\uac00) for new information and -eun/-neun (\uc740/\ub294) for the topic, so the same noun can take either one.",!0,"\uc774",B.bHn,!0,1)
B.QR=s(["\uc744","\uc5d0","\uc774"],t.s)
B.aVM=new A.Q("ko:g-object",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc744/\ub97c","object marker (the thing acted on)","Attach it to the thing that receives the action: -eul (\uc744) after a consonant and -reul (\ub97c) after a vowel (chaek-eul (\ucc45\uc744), keopi-reul (\ucee4\ud53c\ub97c)). The order is object first and verb last, so 'I drink coffee' becomes 'coffee-reul drink'. In casual speech people often drop it, but learners should keep it so the sentence stays clear.","\uc800\ub294 \ucee4\ud53c\ub97c \ub9c8\uc154\uc694.","jeoneun keopireul masyeoyo.","I drink coffee.","TOPIK 1","Don't copy English word order: the verb always comes last, after the object.",!0,"\ub97c",B.QR,!0,1)
B.cWI=s(["\ub97c","\uac00","\uc774"],t.s)
B.aVF=new A.Q("ko:g-at-ni",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc5d0","at / in / to (time, place, destination)","Use it after a time (7 o'clock, weekends), after the place where something is (with isseoyo (\uc788\uc5b4\uc694)), and after the place you go to (hakgyo-e gayo (\ud559\uad50\uc5d0 \uac00\uc694)). It never changes shape, whatever the last sound of the noun is. English uses different words (at, on, in, to), but Korean uses -e (\uc5d0) for all of these, though words like yesterday, today, tomorrow, and now take no particle (eoje (\uc5b4\uc81c), not eoje-e).","\uc800\ub294 \uc77c\uacf1 \uc2dc\uc5d0 \uc77c\uc5b4\ub098\uc694.","jeoneun ilgop sie ireonayo.","I get up at seven.","TOPIK 1","Don't use -e (\uc5d0) for where an action happens, since that needs -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c).",!0,"\uc5d0",B.cWI,!0,1)
B.Sy=s(["\uc5d0","\ub97c","\ub85c"],t.s)
B.aRZ=new A.Q("ko:g-at-eseo",1,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc5d0\uc11c","at / in (where an action happens)","Use it after a place where you do an action, like studying, eating, or working (doseogwan-eseo gongbuhaeyo (\ub3c4\uc11c\uad00\uc5d0\uc11c \uacf5\ubd80\ud574\uc694)). It also means 'from' in some sentences, but at first learn the 'at' meaning. English uses at or in for both places and actions, but Korean splits them: -e (\uc5d0) is for where something is or goes, and -eseo (\uc5d0\uc11c) is for where something is done.","\uc800\ub294 \ub3c4\uc11c\uad00\uc5d0\uc11c \uacf5\ubd80\ud574\uc694.","jeoneun doseogwaneseo gongbuhaeyo.","I study at the library.","TOPIK 1","Compare isseoyo (\uc788\uc5b4\uc694) with -e (it's in the room) and action verbs with -eseo (I study in the room).",!0,"\uc5d0\uc11c",B.Sy,!0,1)
B.cJ5=s(["\uba39\uc544\uc694","\uba39\uc694","\uba39\uc73c\uc694"],t.s)
B.aSI=new A.Q("ko:g-polite-present",2,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544\uc694/\uc5b4\uc694","polite present tense","Use it for everyday polite speech about now, habits, and often the near future. Take the verb or adjective stem and add -ayo (\uc544\uc694) if its last vowel is a or o (joh-ayo (\uc88b\uc544\uc694)), and -eoyo (\uc5b4\uc694) for all other vowels (meog-eoyo (\uba39\uc5b4\uc694)); hada verbs become haeyo. The same form works for statements and questions, so only your tone changes, and it doesn't change for I, you, or they like English verbs do.","\uc800\ub294 \ub9e4\uc77c \ubc25\uc744 \uba39\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun maeil babeul meogeoyo.","I eat rice every day.","TOPIK 1","The vowel rule only looks at the last vowel of the stem, so it's -ayo (\uc544\uc694) for a and o and -eoyo (\uc5b4\uc694) for everything else.",!0,"\uba39\uc5b4\uc694",B.cJ5,!0,1)
B.yE=s(["\uc54a","\uc548\uc774","\uc5c6"],t.s)
B.aTn=new A.Q("ko:g-an-neg",3,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc548 + \ub3d9\uc0ac/\ud615\uc6a9\uc0ac","short negation (don't / isn't)","Put an (\uc548) right before a verb or adjective to say you don't do something or something isn't so (an meogeoyo (\uc548 \uba39\uc5b4\uc694)). It's the short, spoken negative, and it works for normal verbs and adjectives alike. With hada verbs, put it between the noun and haeyo: gongbu-reul an haeyo (\uacf5\ubd80\ub97c \uc548 \ud574\uc694). Don't mix it up with mot (\ubabb), which means can't.","\uc800\ub294 \ucee4\ud53c\ub97c \uc548 \ub9c8\uc154\uc694.","jeoneun keopireul an masyeoyo.","I don't drink coffee.","TOPIK 1","An (\uc548) goes in front of the verb, not at the end of the sentence like English not.",!0,"\uc548",B.yE,!0,1)
B.bTy=s(["\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc548\uc544\uc694","\ub9c8\uc2dc \uc54a\uc544\uc694","\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc5b4\uc694"],t.s)
B.aYj=new A.Q("ko:g-jianayo",3,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694","long negation (don't / isn't)","Attach -ji anayo (\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694) to the verb or adjective stem (mashi-ji anayo (\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694), chup-ji anayo (\ucda5\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694)). It means the same as the short an (\uc548) negative but sounds a little more formal, and people use it a lot in writing. It's spelled with an h (anh-), but the h is silent, so it sounds like anayo.","\uc800\ub294 \uc6b0\uc720\ub97c \ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694.","jeoneun uyureul masiji anayo.","I don't drink milk.","TOPIK 1","Use the bare stem before -ji (\uc9c0), not the polite form (mashi-ji anayo (\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694), not masyeoyo-ji anayo).",!0,"\ub9c8\uc2dc\uc9c0 \uc54a\uc544\uc694",B.bTy,!0,1)
B.cI4=s(["\ub9cc\ub0ac\uc544\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098\uc558\uc5b4\uc694"],t.s)
B.aTs=new A.Q("ko:g-past",4,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc558\uc5b4\uc694/\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694","polite past tense","Use it to say something already happened. Take the stem and add -ass-eoyo (\uc558\uc5b4\uc694) if the last vowel is a or o, and -eoss-eoyo (\uc5c8\uc5b4\uc694) for other vowels; hada verbs become haess-eoyo (\ud588\uc5b4\uc694). English changes the verb to the past (ate, went), but Korean adds this ending to the stem, and it stays the same for I, you, and they. After a vowel, the stem and ass merge into one syllable: manna + ass-eoyo = mannass-eoyo (\ub9cc\ub0ac\uc5b4\uc694).","\uc5b4\uc81c \uce5c\uad6c\ub97c \ub9cc\ub0ac\uc5b4\uc694.","eoje chingureul mannasseoyo.","I met a friend yesterday.","TOPIK 1","The ss comes before the polite ending, so don't just add -eoyo (\uc5b4\uc694) to the present form.",!0,"\ub9cc\ub0ac\uc5b4\uc694",B.cI4,!0,1)
B.cZO=s(["\ub9cc\ub098\uc744 \uac70\uc608\uc694","\ub9cc\ub0a0 \uac70\uc5d0\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098 \uac70\uc608\uc694"],t.s)
B.aWl=new A.Q("ko:g-future",4,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139 \uac70\uc608\uc694","will / going to (plans and guesses)","Use it for plans and for guesses, like I'm going to... or it will... After a vowel stem add -l geoyeyo (man-nal geoyeyo (\ub9cc\ub0a0 \uac70\uc608\uc694)), and after a consonant stem add -eul geoyeyo (\uc744 \uac70\uc608\uc694) (ilg-eul geoyeyo (\uc77d\uc744 \uac70\uc608\uc694)). Stems that already end in l just add geoyeyo (sal geoyeyo (\uc0b4 \uac70\uc608\uc694)). English uses will or going to, but Korean keeps the verb and only changes the ending.","\ub0b4\uc77c \uce5c\uad6c\ub97c \ub9cc\ub0a0 \uac70\uc608\uc694.","naeil chingureul mannal geoyeyo.","I'm going to meet a friend tomorrow.","TOPIK 1","The standard spelling is geoyeyo (\uac70\uc608\uc694), and the common spelling geoeyo (\uac70\uc5d0\uc694) is nonstandard, so don't use it.",!0,"\ub9cc\ub0a0 \uac70\uc608\uc694",B.cZO,!0,1)
B.N5=s(["\uc788\uc544\uc694","\uc788\uc694","\uc788\uc73c\uc694"],t.s)
B.aTb=new A.Q("ko:g-iss-eops",5,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc788\uc5b4\uc694/\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694","there is / there isn't; to have / not have","Use isseoyo (\uc788\uc5b4\uc694) for 'there is' and 'I have', and eopseoyo (\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694) for 'there isn't' and 'I don't have'. Say the place or the owner first, then the thing with -i/-ga (\uc774/\uac00): jib-e goyangi-ga isseoyo (\uc9d1\uc5d0 \uace0\uc591\uc774\uac00 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694). Korean usually has no separate word for have, so these two words do the job. Don't make the negative with an, because eopseoyo is its own opposite.","\uc9d1\uc5d0 \uace0\uc591\uc774\uac00 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","jibe goyangiga isseoyo.","There's a cat at home.","TOPIK 1","The negative of isseoyo (\uc788\uc5b4\uc694) is eopseoyo (\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694), not an isseoyo.",!0,"\uc788\uc5b4\uc694",B.N5,!0,1)
B.cus=s(["\uc774\uac83","\uc774\uac8c","\uc774\uac70"],t.s)
B.aY7=new A.Q("ko:g-demonstrative",6,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc774/\uadf8/\uc800 + \uba85\uc0ac","this / that / that over there + noun","Put i (\uc774), geu (\uadf8), or jeo (\uc800) right before a noun: i chaek (\uc774 \ucc45, this book), geu chaek (that book), jeo chaek (that book over there). I means near the speaker, geu means near the listener or something you both just mentioned, and jeo means far from both. English has two (this, that) but Korean has three, and there is no word for a or the.","\uc774 \ucc45\uc740 \uc7ac\ubbf8\uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","i chaegeun jaemiisseoyo.","This book is fun.","TOPIK 1","Don't add a particle between the word and the noun, so it's i chaek (\uc774 \ucc45), not i-neun chaek.",!0,"\uc774",B.cus,!0,1)
B.dej=s(["\uba39\uc5b4\uace0","\uba39\uc73c\uace0","\uba39\ub294\uace0"],t.s)
B.aTd=new A.Q("ko:g-and-go",7,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uace0","and / and then (joining two actions)",'Attach -go (\uace0) straight to the verb stem to join two actions in order, like "and" or "and then" in English. It works the same with or without a final consonant, so you never change the stem. In "and then" sequences, the tense and the polite ending usually go only on the last verb.',"\uc800\ub294 \ubc25\uc744 \uba39\uace0 \ucee4\ud53c\ub97c \ub9c8\uc154\uc694.","jeoneun babeul meokgo keopireul masyeoyo.","I eat a meal and then drink coffee.","TOPIK 1","Attach -go (\uace0) directly to the stem, and usually put the tense only on the last verb.",!0,"\uba39\uace0",B.dej,!0,1)
B.dm0=s(["\uce5c\uad6c\ub97c\ud558\uace0","\uce5c\uad6c\uc640\uace0","\uce5c\uad6c\uc5d0\ud558\uace0"],t.s)
B.aUl=new A.Q("ko:g-with-hago",7,"\ucd08\uae09","\ud558\uace0 / \uc640 / \uacfc","and, with (joining nouns)",'Put hago (\ud558\uace0), wa (\uc640) or gwa (\uacfc) between two nouns to say "A and B," or after a person to say "with." Hago works after any noun and is the easiest and most common in speech. Wa follows a word that ends in a vowel and gwa follows a word that ends in a consonant, and they sound a bit more formal or written.',"\uc800\ub294 \uce5c\uad6c\ud558\uace0 \uc601\ud654\ub97c \ubd10\uc694.","jeoneun chinguhago yeonghwareul bwayo.","I watch a movie with a friend.","TOPIK 1","Use wa (\uc640) after a vowel and gwa (\uacfc) after a consonant, but hago (\ud558\uace0) works after anything.",!0,"\uce5c\uad6c\ud558\uace0",B.dm0,!0,1)
B.d9v=s(["\ucee4\ud53c\ub3c4\ub97c","\ucee4\ud53c\ub97c\ub3c4","\ucee4\ud53c\uac00\ub3c4"],t.s)
B.aVy=new A.Q("ko:g-also-do",7,"\ucd08\uae09","\ub3c4","also, too",'Add -do (\ub3c4) to a noun to say "also" or "too" about that noun. It takes the place of the particles i/ga (\uc774/\uac00), eun/neun (\uc740/\ub294) and eul/reul (\uc744/\ub97c), so don\'t stack them (not "coffee-do-reul"). It has the same form after a vowel or a consonant, and it attaches right after the noun it points to.',"\uc800\ub294 \ucee4\ud53c\ub3c4 \uc88b\uc544\ud574\uc694.","jeoneun keopido joahaeyo.","I like coffee too.","TOPIK 1","The particle -do (\ub3c4) replaces the subject and object particles instead of sitting next to them.",!0,"\ucee4\ud53c\ub3c4",B.d9v,!0,1)
B.bVj=s(["\ud558\ub098 \uac1c","\uc14b \uac1c","\uc0bc \uac1c"],t.s)
B.aVv=new A.Q("ko:g-counters",8,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc218 \uc138\uae30: \uac1c\xb7\uba85\xb7\uc794","counting things and people with counters",'Korean puts a number and a counter after the noun: apples "se gae (\uc138 \uac1c)" (3 things), friends "du myeong (\ub450 \uba85)" (2 people), coffee "han jan (\ud55c \uc794)" (1 cup). Use native numbers (hana (\ud558\ub098), dul (\ub458), set (\uc14b), net (\ub137)) in a short form before counters: han, du, se, ne, and then daseot, yeoseot and so on. Use Sino numbers (il (\uc77c), i (\uc774), sam (\uc0bc)) for dates, money, minutes and phone numbers. English doesn\'t need this extra counter word, so it\'s easy to forget.',"\uc0ac\uacfc \uc138 \uac1c\ub97c \uc0c0\uc5b4\uc694.","sagwa se gaereul sasseoyo.","I bought three apples.","TOPIK 1","Native numbers go with counters like gae (\uac1c), myeong (\uba85) and jan (\uc794), and Sino numbers go with money and dates.",!0,"\uc138 \uac1c",B.bVj,!0,1)
B.cEi=s(["\uc77d\uc138\uc694","\uc77d\uc5b4\uc138\uc694","\uc77d\uc2dc\uc694"],t.s)
B.aSZ=new A.Q("ko:g-honorific-seyo",9,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\uc138\uc694","polite honorific present, also a polite request",'Add -seyo (\uc138\uc694) to a verb stem that ends in a vowel, and -euseyo (\uc73c\uc138\uc694) to a stem that ends in a consonant, to show respect to the person doing the action. Use it for teachers, parents, elders or strangers, and never for yourself. It also works as a soft request or command, like "please" in English. Korean often leaves out the subject, so the verb ending shows who you\'re respecting.',"\uc120\uc0dd\ub2d8\uc740 \uc9c0\uae08 \ucc45\uc744 \uc77d\uc73c\uc138\uc694.","seonsaengnimeun jigeum chaegeul ilgeuseyo.","The teacher is reading a book now.","TOPIK 1","Never use -seyo (\uc138\uc694) for your own actions, only for the person you respect.",!0,"\uc77d\uc73c\uc138\uc694",B.cEi,!0,1)
B.cek=s(["\ub9d0\ud558 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694","\ub9d0\ud574 \uc8fc\uc2dc\uc694","\ub9d0\ud574\uc694 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694"],t.s)
B.aVs=new A.Q("ko:g-juseyo",9,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544/\uc5b4 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694","please do (something) for me",'Add -a/eo juseyo (\uc544/\uc5b4 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694) to a verb to ask someone to do something for you, like "please ..." or "could you ..." in English. Take the polite -a/eo form of the verb (open becomes yeoreo (\uc5f4\uc5b4), write becomes sseo (\uc368)) and add juseyo after a space. With a plain noun you can just say the noun plus juseyo, as in "mul juseyo (\ubb3c \uc8fc\uc138\uc694)" for "water, please." It\'s polite enough for shops, restaurants and strangers.',"\ucc9c\ucc9c\ud788 \ub9d0\ud574 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694.","cheoncheonhi malhae juseyo.","Please speak slowly.","TOPIK 1","Use the -a/eo (\uc544/\uc5b4) form before juseyo (\uc8fc\uc138\uc694), not the bare stem.",!0,"\ub9d0\ud574 \uc8fc\uc138\uc694",B.cek,!0,1)
B.bEr=s(["\uac00\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\ud558\uc694","\uac00\uc11c \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694","\uac00\uace0 \uc88b\uc544\uc694"],t.s)
B.aT9=new A.Q("ko:g-want",9,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694","want to (do something)",'Attach -go sipeoyo (\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694) to a verb stem to say you want to do something, like "I want to ..." in English. The stem doesn\'t change, whether it ends in a vowel or a consonant. It\'s for your own wishes, so with "he/she wants to" use -go sipeohaeyo (\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\ud574\uc694). English says "want to go" with a separate verb, but Korean usually attaches an ending to the verb instead.',"\uc800\ub294 \ud55c\uad6d\uc5d0 \uac00\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun hanguge gago sipeoyo.","I want to go to Korea.","TOPIK 1","For another person's wish, use -go sipeohaeyo (\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\ud574\uc694) instead of -go sipeoyo (\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694).",!0,"\uac00\uace0 \uc2f6\uc5b4\uc694",B.bEr,!0,1)
B.dfr=s(["\uc77d\ub294 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694","\uc77d\uc744 \uc218 \uc788\uc694","\uc77d\uc744 \uc218 \uc774\uc5d0\uc694"],t.s)
B.aV5=new A.Q("ko:g-can",10,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694/\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694","can / cannot (ability or possibility)",'Add -l su isseoyo to a verb stem that ends in a vowel, or -eul su isseoyo (\uc744 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694) to one that ends in a consonant, to say you can do something. Use eopseoyo (\uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694) instead of isseoyo to say you can\'t. It covers both "I know how to" and "it\'s possible to," which English splits into can and be able to. Stems that end in the letter l (like "nolda (\ub180\ub2e4)," to play) keep it and just take su, as in nol su isseoyo (\ub180 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694).',"\uc800\ub294 \ud55c\uad6d\uc5b4\ub97c \uc77d\uc744 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun hangugeoreul ilgeul su isseoyo.","I can read Korean.","TOPIK 1","Use su isseoyo (\uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694) for can and su eopseoyo (\uc218 \uc5c6\uc5b4\uc694) for cannot, and keep su separate with spaces.",!0,"\uc77d\uc744 \uc218 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694",B.dfr,!0,1)
B.c2e=s(["\uba39 \ubabb \ud574\uc694","\uba39\uc5b4 \ubabb\uc694","\ubabb \uba39\ub294\uc694"],t.s)
B.aSG=new A.Q("ko:g-mot",10,"\ucd08\uae09","\ubabb + \ub3d9\uc0ac","can't / unable to (before a verb)",'Put mot (\ubabb) right before a verb to say you can\'t do it, because of ability or circumstances. It differs from an (just "don\'t"): mot means you want to or should but can\'t. With verbs like "hada" (to do), it goes between the noun and the verb, as in "suyeong mot haeyo (\uc218\uc601 \ubabb \ud574\uc694)." It\'s a short, common way to say "can\'t" in everyday talk.',"\uc800\ub294 \ub9e4\uc6b4 \uc74c\uc2dd\uc744 \ubabb \uba39\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun maeun eumsigeul mot meogeoyo.","I can't eat spicy food.","TOPIK 1","Mot (\ubabb) goes right before the verb, so for 'noun + hada' put it between the noun and hada.",!0,"\ubabb \uba39\uc5b4\uc694",B.c2e,!0,1)
B.bSJ=s(["\uce5c\uad6c\uc5d0","\uce5c\uad6c\ub97c","\uce5c\uad6c\uc5d0\uc11c"],t.s)
B.aTM=new A.Q("ko:g-to-person",11,"\ucd08\uae09","\uc5d0\uac8c / \ud55c\ud14c","to (a person or animal)",'Put hante (\ud55c\ud14c, casual and common in speech) or ege (\uc5d0\uac8c, a bit more written) after a person to mean "to" them, like when giving, calling or writing. Use e (\uc5d0) for places and things, not people ("to school," not "to a friend"). For someone you want to show respect to, such as a teacher or grandparent, use kke (\uaed8). English often drops "to" (give me a gift), but Korean always needs this particle.',"\uc800\ub294 \uce5c\uad6c\ud55c\ud14c \uc120\ubb3c\uc744 \uc92c\uc5b4\uc694.","jeoneun chinguhante seonmureul jwosseoyo.","I gave a gift to my friend.","TOPIK 1","Use hante (\ud55c\ud14c) or ege (\uc5d0\uac8c) for people and animals, and e (\uc5d0) only for places and things.",!0,"\uce5c\uad6c\ud55c\ud14c",B.bSJ,!0,1)
B.drU=s(["\ub9cc\ub098\ub85c \uac00\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098\ub7ec\uc11c \uac00\uc694","\ub9cc\ub098\uace0\ub7ec \uac00\uc694"],t.s)
B.aSc=new A.Q("ko:g-go-to-do",11,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\ub7ec \uac00\uc694","go in order to (do)",'Add -reo (\ub7ec) to a verb stem that ends in a vowel, or -eureo (\uc73c\ub7ec) to one that ends in a consonant, and then gayo (\uac00\uc694) (or oayo (\uc640\uc694)) to say you go somewhere to do something. Stems that end in the letter l take -reo, like "nolda" becoming "nolreo gayo (\ub180\ub7ec \uac00\uc694)." A movement verb such as go or come has to follow it. English says "go to see" or "go and see," while Korean uses this one pattern.',"\uc800\ub294 \uce5c\uad6c\ub97c \ub9cc\ub098\ub7ec \uac00\uc694.","jeoneun chingureul mannareo gayo.","I'm going to meet a friend.","TOPIK 1","Only a go or come verb can follow -(eu)reo.",!0,"\ub9cc\ub098\ub7ec \uac00\uc694",B.drU,!0,1)
B.dqX=s(["\uba39\uc744\uac00\uc694","\uba39\ub294\uae4c\uc694","\uba39\uc744\uae4c\uc544\uc694"],t.s)
B.aW1=new A.Q("ko:g-shall-we",12,"\ucd08\uae09","-(\uc73c)\u3139\uae4c\uc694?","shall we...? / should I...?",'Add -lkkayo to a verb stem that ends in a vowel, or -eulkkayo (\uc744\uae4c\uc694) to one that ends in a consonant, to suggest something or offer to do something, like "Shall we ...?" or "Should I ...?" in English. Stems that end in the letter l just add -kkayo, such as "yeolda (\uc5f4\ub2e4)" becoming "yeolkkayo (\uc5f4\uae4c\uc694)." The usual answer is "joayo (\uc88b\uc544\uc694)" (sounds good). It can also mean "I wonder if ..." about something you\'re unsure of.',"\uac19\uc774 \uc810\uc2ec \uba39\uc744\uae4c\uc694?","gachi jeomsim meogeulkkayo?","Shall we have lunch together?","TOPIK 1","It's a suggestion or an offer, so the usual answer is joayo (\uc88b\uc544\uc694) or ne (\ub124).",!0,"\uba39\uc744\uae4c\uc694",B.dqX,!0,1)
B.bNE=s(["\uc624\uc544\uc11c","\uc624\uc5b4\uc11c","\uc654\uc5b4\uc11c"],t.s)
B.aVE=new A.Q("ko:g-because-aseo",12,"\ucd08\uae09","-\uc544\uc11c/\uc5b4\uc11c","because, so (reason)",'Add -aseo (\uc544\uc11c) after a verb or adjective stem whose last vowel is a or o, and -eoseo (\uc5b4\uc11c) after any other stem. Verbs that end in hada become haeseo (\ud574\uc11c), and stems ending in eu (like gopeuda) drop it and follow the vowel before it (gopaseo (\uace0\ud30c\uc11c)). It gives the reason, like "because" or "so" in English, and the reason comes first. Don\'t put the past tense inside it (not -asseoseo or -eosseoseo), and don\'t follow it with a command or "let\'s" (the result part can\'t be a request).',"\ube44\uac00 \uc640\uc11c \uc9d1\uc5d0 \uc788\uc5b4\uc694.","biga waseo jibe isseoyo.","It's raining, so I stay home.","TOPIK 1","Keep the reason in the present form, and don't follow it with a command or a suggestion.",!0,"\uc640\uc11c",B.bNE,!0,1)
